{
 "id": "2026-10-28-toi561b-shiny-lava-world",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "A planet covered in lava may be hiding behind shiny clouds. How?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Astronomers pointed the Webb telescope at a planet called TOI five sixty one b for thirty seven hours, nearly four orbits. It circles so close to its star that its year lasts less than an Earth day. Its temperature is estimated at two thousand three hundred kelvin."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "A team led by the University of Waterloo reports in The Astronomical Journal that the planet reflects about three quarters of its starlight, comparable to Venus. The night side is too cool to explain the missing heat. Models point to bright silicate clouds."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "The researchers say the air could be thick with carbon dioxide, like a hotter Venus, or rich in sulphur dioxide, like the volcanic moon Io. The composition is unresolved, and the clouds are a model explanation."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Webb confirm what these clouds are made of? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
